# OS / editor cruft
.DS_Store
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# LaTeX build intermediates (the .tex source and final .pdf are tracked;
# these are pure regenerable byproducts of running pdflatex) -- same
# convention as the sibling PFM-STM32G474 project's .gitignore
*.aux
*.log
*.out
*.toc
*.fls
*.fdb_latexmk
*.synctex.gz

# Debug/ build BYPRODUCTS -- correction, 2026-09-23: the note that used
# to be here said "Debug/ build artifacts are NOT ignored here", full
# stop. That was too broad: it meant every rebuild -- even a one-line
# source edit -- regenerated a ~69,000-line disassembly listing
# (WHAM-XREX-PFMG474.list) that then dominated every diff, to the point
# a cloud code-review tool refused to run on an otherwise-normal commit
# (85,580 of its ~86k changed lines were .list/.map, not source). These
# are pure derived text (objdump/ld output), regenerable from a clean
# build off the tracked .elf, with zero information not already in it.
# Narrowed to actually ignore them; .bin/.elf/subdir.mk are NOT touched
# by this and stay tracked -- see the reasoning below.
#
# *** REAL BUG, FOUND AND FIXED 2026-09-23, one day after this section
# was first added ***: `Debug/**/*.list` (etc.) never actually matched
# the two files most responsible for the original problem --
# Debug/WHAM-XREX-PFMG474.list/.map sit directly in Debug/, zero
# subdirectories deep, and this git's glob treats `**/` as requiring
# at least one directory level, not "zero or more" as might be
# assumed -- so the exact 69,000-line file that broke ultrareview in
# the first place stayed tracked the whole time; only the Core/Src/*
# subdirectory intermediates were ever actually caught. Confirmed via
# `git ls-files`/`git check-ignore` after the fact, not assumed. Added
# explicit top-level patterns alongside the `**` ones so both depths
# are covered regardless of this glob quirk.
Debug/*.list
Debug/*.map
Debug/*.o
Debug/*.d
Debug/*.su
Debug/*.cyclo
Debug/**/*.list
Debug/**/*.map
Debug/**/*.o
Debug/**/*.d
Debug/**/*.su
Debug/**/*.cyclo
Debug/objects.list

# Still deliberately tracked, not ignored: Debug/**/*.bin and *.elf
# (the actual flashable artifact -- the meaningful "what's currently on
# the board" record, especially for a dirty-tree build, see
# Core/Inc/git_version.h's own comment below) and Debug/**/subdir.mk
# (semi-manually-maintained when a new source file is added to the
# build -- see AGENTS.md's own "shared-filename"/subdir.mk gotcha note
# -- not purely regenerable by a clean rebuild without that manual
# step, unlike everything ignored just above).

# python/wham_console.py's own generated output -- per-shot CSV/PNG/
# JSON (shots/) and session transcripts (logs/), created on demand via
# os.makedirs(); pure operator-session data, not source, and would
# grow unbounded if tracked. See that script's own header comment.
shots/
logs/

# Core/Inc/git_version.h -- generated by python/gen_git_version.py
# (called automatically by python/wham_build.py) immediately before
# every build, baking the current commit hash + dirty-tree flag into
# *IDN?'s reply (cmd_idn(), commands.c). Deliberately NOT tracked: its
# whole job is to capture "what commit was THIS build made from," which
# is inherently redundant with git's own history for a clean build --
# a fresh clone always regenerates it correctly for HEAD, and if it
# were tracked, every single commit would spuriously modify it (the
# baked-in hash changes the instant you commit). The one case that
# genuinely isn't reconstructable from git alone -- a build made with
# UNCOMMITTED changes (dirty tree) -- isn't lost either: that exact
# state gets baked into the .elf/.bin themselves, which THIS project
# deliberately DOES track in Debug/ (see the NOTE above) -- so the
# information lives in the output artifact, not a separate source file
# that would just create merge noise. Same "regenerable byproduct,
# don't track it" reasoning as the LaTeX intermediates above; see that
# script's own header comment for the exact mechanism.
Core/Inc/git_version.h

# Core/Inc/build_target.h -- generated by python/gen_build_target.py
# (called automatically by python/wham_build.py's --target flag),
# selects CONTROLLER vs SIMULATOR firmware from this same repo, added
# 2026-09-17 for the Transrex-simulator work. Same reasoning as
# git_version.h just above: whichever target you build is a build-time
# choice, not a source-tracked one -- a fresh clone defaults to
# CONTROLLER (gen_build_target.py's own default), and tracking this
# file would make every target switch look like a spurious commit.
Core/Inc/build_target.h
